04 · THEATRE.JS: CINEMATIC SEQUENCE SYSTEM

Use Theatre.js as the preferred authoring environment for cinematic keyframe sequences when it is compatible with the verified project.

Theatre.js and GSAP must not become competing authorities over the same properties.

4.1 Responsibilities

Theatre.js should control authored cinematic properties such as:

* Camera positions and orientations
* Camera targets
* Mesh transforms
* Object entrances and exits
* Lighting changes
* Selected material parameters
* Scene transitions
* Keyframe timing
* Presentation sequencing

GSAP may control appropriately scoped DOM and interface choreography, text transitions, counters and coordinated interaction effects.

Procedural real-time effects may use shader uniforms or shared render-frame updates.

All systems must receive time from a defined timing architecture.

4.2 Sequence architecture

Every cinematic segment must define:

* Sequence ID
* Scene ID
* Start time
* End time
* Duration
* Camera ownership
* Target objects
* Keyframes
* Easing curves
* Shader animations
* Lighting transitions
* Text cues
* Interaction availability
* Audio cues where applicable
* Exit state
* Interruption behavior

Save authored Theatre.js project and sequence state as version-controlled project data.

Do not depend on developer-local editor state as the only source of production animation.

Theatre.js Studio should be available only in authorized development or editing environments.

4.3 Playback control

Provide explicit operations for:

* Play
* Pause
* Resume
* Seek
* Restart
* Reverse where supported
* Cancel
* Transition to another sequence
* Restore interactive control

Store the active sequence and playback position.

Do not create unmanaged looping timelines.

Sequence completion must provide an explicit state transition.

4.4 Motion continuity

Transitions must preserve the final spatial state of the outgoing scene unless a deliberately designed visual transition changes it.

No unexplained camera reset.

No instantaneous object teleportation.

No sudden material replacement.

No unexpected lighting discontinuity.

No independent animation playing over a completed transition.

For presentations intended to resemble a continuous shot, maintain a single coherent spatial world and choreograph camera, lighting and subject transitions inside it.

