05 · CAMERA CINEMATOGRAPHY SYSTEM

5.1 Camera direction

The camera must behave like a directed virtual cinematography system.

Support:

* Dolly in and out
* Truck left and right
* Pedestal up and down
* Crane-style movement
* Orbit
* Pan
* Tilt
* Arc shots
* Tracking shots
* Parallax reveals
* Focus-target transitions
* Spline-based movement
* Smooth position and orientation interpolation

Use camera-controls, Drei CameraControls, a purpose-built camera rig, or an approved existing equivalent.

Select one authoritative camera control architecture.

5.2 Continuous camera movement

Implement camera trajectories through suitable interpolation techniques.

Use:

* Bézier curves
* Catmull-Rom splines
* Quaternion interpolation
* Smooth damping
* Controlled ease-in/ease-out
* Explicit look-at targets
* Optional focal-offset animation

Preserve continuity in position, velocity, orientation and target behavior when joining shots.

Do not assume a visually smooth camera path is achieved merely by increasing animation duration.

Evaluate transition curves and their joins.

5.3 Camera shot contract

Every shot or camera move must document:

Property	Specification
Shot ID	Unique identifier
Start / end	Sequence time
Duration	Seconds
Start position	XYZ
End position	XYZ
Start / end orientation	Quaternion or derived target
Camera target	World-space reference
FOV	Degrees
Camera trajectory	Curve or control method
Easing	Named/configured curve
Subject	Component ID
Focus behavior	Explicit or none
Transition	Entry and exit state
Ownership	Camera controller ID

5.4 Cinematic choreography

Each movement must communicate something.

The camera can:

* Introduce a spatial environment.
* Reveal a hidden component.
* Establish relationships between objects.
* Follow a meaningful data connection.
* Transition from a 3D object into an infographic.
* Move through a glass interface.
* Reframe around an expanding chart.
* Transition from wide composition to editorial close-up.

Avoid constant orbiting solely to make a scene appear dynamic.

The camera should lead the viewer’s attention.

5.5 User control handoff

Define explicit modes:

* Cinematic playback
* Interactive exploration
* Transitioning to interaction
* Returning to presentation
* Paused
* Error / recovery

During cinematic playback, user input must follow the approved interruption policy.

During interactive exploration, user control must not fight the cinematic camera timeline.

On returning to playback, use a designed transition from the current camera position to the next cinematic state.

