06 · CUSTOM GLSL SHADERS AND REALISTIC MATERIALS

The material system must combine physically plausible behavior with restrained custom shader techniques.

6.1 Material philosophy

Create convincing optical qualities rather than generic shiny surfaces.

Depending on the approved assets, material families may include:

* Optical glass
* Frosted glass
* Clear coated metal
* Brushed aluminium
* Dark metallic surfaces
* Translucent resin
* Thin-film interference
* Fine procedural surface detail
* Subtle emissive elements
* Atmospheric materials

Do not introduce a material family simply because it is technically impressive.

6.2 Physically based rendering

Use compatible PBR materials such as MeshPhysicalMaterial where appropriate.

Configure material properties according to actual reference assets and physical intent:

* Roughness
* Metalness
* Transmission
* Index of refraction
* Thickness
* Clearcoat
* Environment intensity
* Emissive strength
* Normal detail
* Transparency
* Attenuation parameters

Maintain physically coherent color management.

Color maps normally require sRGB interpretation; non-color data maps must be treated as data, not display color.

Verify imported texture channel packing and model orientation.

6.3 Custom shader architecture

Use GLSL for effects that are not adequately achievable with conventional material parameters.

Potential approved applications include:

* Procedural micro-surface variation
* Controlled energy patterns
* Optical distortion
* Fresnel-based appearance
* Depth-dependent opacity
* Subtle displacement
* Time-based surface animation
* Procedural noise
* Highlight modulation
* Special transition masks

Each shader must expose a documented uniform schema.

Example uniform categories:

* Time
* Progress
* Intensity
* Primary/secondary color
* Roughness adjustment
* Distortion magnitude
* Noise scale
* Fresnel strength
* Opacity
* Resolution

Use physically compatible shader customization where possible.

Do not replace a correct PBR lighting workflow with an unlit custom shader merely to create a visual effect.

6.4 Shader control

Shader animation should derive from the authoritative motion clock or explicitly documented real-time state.

Avoid each shader creating its own unmanaged timing source.

Support:

* Pause and resume
* Deterministic seeking
* Controlled transitions
* Reduced-motion variants
* Debugging of uniforms
* Cleanup on component disposal

Test on Safari and Chrome.

