07 · CINEMATIC LIGHTING

Lighting must shape the composition, reveal materials and provide visual continuity.

7.1 Lighting structure

Define separate controllable lighting responsibilities:

* Key light
* Fill light
* Rim/back light
* Environment illumination
* Approved accent lights
* Emissive object sources
* Contact shadows or other supported grounding techniques

Use HDR environment lighting and PMREM where compatible and justified.

Avoid adding multiple expensive real-time lights without measuring their effect.

7.2 Lighting choreography

Permit controlled animation of:

* Light position
* Light color
* Intensity
* Direction
* Environment rotation where supported
* Exposure-related presentation settings
* Approved accent lighting

Light changes must synchronize with the cinematic timeline.

7.3 Visual discipline

Avoid:

* Overexposed highlights
* Plastic-looking glass
* Excessive colored lighting
* Inconsistent environmental reflections
* Unmotivated flashing
* Heavy halos around every object
* Unreadable dark materials

Lighting should reveal the material quality without overwhelming the design.

