08 · POSTPROCESSING AND OPTICAL FINISH

Use a unified EffectComposer-based postprocessing system compatible with the existing renderer.

8.1 Supported effects to evaluate

* Selective Bloom
* Depth of Field
* Bokeh
* Film Grain / Noise
* Vignette
* Ambient Occlusion
* Antialiasing
* Color grading
* Controlled atmospheric depth
* Motion blur, only where technically justified and stable

Effects must be enabled individually through a defined configuration.

Do not apply every effect by default.

8.2 Suggested initial art-direction ranges

The following are exploratory tuning targets, not approved design tokens:

Effect	Initial direction
Bloom	Low intensity, restricted to highlights
Grain	Very subtle, avoid masking text
Vignette	Low-to-moderate edge treatment
Depth of Field	Selective and primarily cinematic
Ambient Occlusion	Subtle grounding
Chromatic aberration	Disabled unless explicitly required
Motion blur	Disabled by default until validated
Sharpening	Avoid halos and artificial edges

Tune using visual references and device comparisons.

No postprocessing may make typography, charts or UI controls difficult to read.

8.3 Postprocessing ownership

Maintain one authoritative pipeline per intended rendered scene.

Individual components may request effect presets or effect parameters through explicit APIs.

They must not instantiate uncontrolled competing composers.

Ensure postprocessing resize behavior matches the render viewport and pixel ratio.

Test correct color-space output and antialiasing behavior.

