10 · DATA VISUALIZATION AND MOTION GRAPHICS

10.1 Information-design approach

Avoid text-heavy dashboards.

Communicate information through:

* Radial diagrams
* Data rings
* Timelines
* Network diagrams
* Animated bar charts
* Line charts
* Comparison graphics
* Progress systems
* KPI compositions
* Process diagrams
* Spatial data relationships
* 3D models where genuinely useful

Use a charting technology compatible with the existing application, such as SVG-based React components, an approved charting library or custom WebGL visualization.

The selected approach must serve clarity, not technical spectacle.

10.2 Meaningful visual geometry

Use shapes with distinct purposes:

Circles and rings: cycles, proportions, stages and systems.

Lines and connections: relationships, transitions and data flows.

Bars and columns: measurable comparisons.

Spheres: spatial identities, systems or three-dimensional data relationships.

Planes and panels: supporting information and interaction surfaces.

Paths: progression, chronology and process flow.

Do not invent decorative data relationships.

Every visualization must have a semantic connection to its content.

10.3 Motion graphics

Implement animation such as:

* Chart reveals
* Sequential bar growth
* Numeric counters
* Diagram construction
* Data-point connections
* Geometric transformations
* Typography reveals
* Depth-based panel transitions
* Morphing between compatible shapes
* Camera-driven infographic transitions

Preserve correct underlying values during animation.

A chart should not communicate a false intermediate value or misleading scale.

10.4 Editorial typography

Use the exact approved fonts.

For English, apply the approved brand display typography where specified.

For Arabic, use a correctly shaped, approved Arabic-capable typeface.

Provide:

* Large display headlines
* Bold, concise supporting statements
* Controlled typography scale
* High-contrast data labels
* Consistent baseline alignment
* Legible numerical treatments
* Clear distinction between captions and functional controls

