11 · ARABIC-FIRST VISUAL QUALITY AND RTL

Arabic must receive the same design quality and creative direction as English.

Arabic is not an afterthought, mirrored English screenshot or automatic translation layer.

11.1 Linguistic requirements

Use natural, professionally written Arabic appropriate to the selected audience.

Support a configurable content locale:

* English
* Arabic
* Arabic regional variants when approved

Do not assume Egyptian, Najdi or formal Arabic are interchangeable.

Do not invent Arabic translations of brand names or technical terms when approved spellings already exist.

11.2 Typography requirements

* Use a typeface with proper Arabic glyph coverage.
* Preserve Arabic shaping and joining.
* Use appropriate line height and visual weight.
* Avoid letter spacing that breaks connected Arabic script.
* Preserve punctuation and word boundaries.
* Support Arabic diacritics where required.
* Ensure numbers and Latin abbreviations display correctly.
* Apply font fallbacks explicitly and verify their visual quality.

Arabic display headlines must feel editorial, bold and intentional.

Do not use generic fallback typography when the approved design requires something more specific.

11.3 RTL engineering

Use semantic HTML language and direction attributes.

Apply lang="ar" and dir="rtl" to appropriate Arabic content containers.

Use logical CSS properties such as:

* margin-inline-start
* margin-inline-end
* padding-inline-start
* padding-inline-end
* inset-inline-start
* text-align: start

Use bidirectional text isolation for mixed Arabic, English, numbers, dates, URLs and technical identifiers.

Do not reverse data order, chronological charts or numerical axes automatically merely because the UI is RTL.

Determine direction based on information semantics.

11.4 Arabic motion graphics

Arabic text animation must respect word integrity and letter shaping.

Prefer word-level, line-level, mask-based or container-level animation when letter-level animation would disrupt joining.

Implement proper wrapping and animation bounds for changing text widths.

Avoid clipped ascenders, descenders and punctuation.

Test actual Arabic screens, not placeholder Lorem Ipsum.

11.5 RTL quality control

Verify:

* Arabic shaping
* Real text, not broken glyphs
* Correct numeral direction
* Mixed Arabic/English ordering
* RTL controls
* Chart labels
* Caption alignment
* Text wrapping
* Visual hierarchy
* Landscape and portrait layouts
* Screenshot and video capture

English and Arabic modes must achieve equivalent visual quality, though their precise compositions may differ.

