12 · RESPONSIVE RENDERING AND PERFORMANCE

12.1 Supported environments

Validate on:

* Desktop Chrome
* Desktop Safari
* Android Chrome
* iPhone Safari
* iPad Safari
* iPad Chrome
* Portrait orientation
* Landscape orientation
* Tablet split-screen
* Resized browser windows

Do not claim support for an environment that has not been tested.

12.2 Pixel ratio and resizing

Use device-aware, performance-aware DPR management.

Configure rendering resolution separately from CSS layout dimensions.

Update render targets, camera aspect, postprocessing and interaction coordinates whenever the rendering container changes.

Use the container’s actual dimensions, rather than assuming the application always fills the device screen.

Support dynamic viewport units, safe-area insets and ResizeObserver-based layout recalculation where appropriate.

12.3 Performance optimization

Implement measured optimization strategies:

* Adaptive pixel ratio
* Texture compression
* Geometry optimization
* Asset preloading
* Lazy loading
* Frustum culling
* Instancing where useful
* Shader complexity management
* Controlled light/shadow budgets
* Postprocessing quality presets
* Draw-call reduction
* GPU memory cleanup
* Context-loss recovery
* Render-loop scheduling

Maintain visual quality without forcing every device to use identical internal rendering settings.

12.4 Performance targets

Proposed initial evaluation targets:

* Desktop: aim for a stable 60 FPS where feasible.
* Mobile: aim for 60 FPS on capable devices, with a validated 30 FPS fallback.
* Avoid persistent frame-time spikes during camera transitions.
* Avoid sustained memory growth during repeated navigation.
* Keep typography and interactions responsive while assets load.

These are engineering targets, not guarantees.

Record real measurements with the tested device, viewport, resolution scale and scene.

Do not fake successful performance by hiding objects or disabling essential approved features without documenting the tradeoff.

